/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Start with physical absorption.
   - Now let A react first order in the liquid, rate = k·CA with k in s⁻¹.
   - E is the enhancement factor, dimensionless and ≥ 1; NA is the molar flux of A in mol/(m²·s); both fluxes are taken at the same interfacial concentration and the same physical kL.
   - Keep two effects apart.
   - Ha is the Hatta number (dimensionless); k is the first-order rate constant in s⁻¹; DA is the liquid diffusivity of A in m²/s; kL is the physical liquid film coefficient in m/s, the value you would have with no reaction.
   - The film thickness is roughly δ = DA/kL, so Ha ≈ √(k·δ²/DA) = δ ÷ √(DA/k).
   - E is the enhancement factor for a first-order reaction in the film; tanh is the hyperbolic tangent.
   - The fast pseudo-first-order result needs 3 < Ha << E∞.
   - Carbon dioxide scrubbing with aqueous monoethanolamine (MEA) is the standard example.
   - Hydrogen sulphide in dilute caustic soda behaves differently.
*/
