/* book exam traps: Using the gas-side coefficient in Ha. kL and DA are liquid-side values; putting kG or a gas diffusivity gives a meaningless number. | Unit slips: k in s⁻¹, DA in m²/s, kL in m/s. Mixing cm²/s with m/s shifts Ha by a factor of 100, because 1 cm²/s = 10⁻⁴ m²/s and the square root halves that power of ten. A factor of 100 can flip the regime. | Quoting E ≈ Ha everywhere. It holds only for 3 < Ha << E∞; below use E = Ha/tanh(Ha); at the limit use E∞ = 1 + (DB·CB)/(b·DA·CAi). | Treating E as a ratio of measured kL values from two runs. E is a flux ratio at the same CAi and physical kL. | Thinking a slow reaction is useless because E ≈ 1. It keeps CAb near zero, so the full driving force survives — a bulk effect, not film enhancement. */
